
== research: reading about the topic on the web
  | **HOW IT WORKS**
  | 1. An amplifier sends a varying audio current through the voice coil.
  | 2. The coil sits in the gap of a fixed permanent magnet.
  | 3. The current makes the coil a variable electromagnet. The Lorentz force on it is F = B·L·i.
  | 4. The force pushes the coil forward or back, and the direction follows the current direction.
  | 5. The coil is glued to the cone, so the cone moves with it. The spider and surround suspension act as a spring that pulls it back to rest.
  | 6. The cone alternately compresses and rarefies the air, and that is sound.
  | 
  | **REAL PARTS**
  | - 8Ω 0.5W mini speaker
  | - MAX98357A I2S class-D amp module (Adafruit, DFRobot DFR0954): about 3.2W into 4Ω, 1.8W into 8Ω, 2.7–5.5V supply
  | - PAM8403 2×3W analog stereo amp module
  | 
  | **MYTH**
  | - Myth: the magnet moves the cone.
  | - Truth: the permanent magnet stays fixed. The coil's changing current makes it push against that fixed field, and the coil moves the cone.
  | 
  | **SURPRISING FACT**
  | - One MIT report says dynamic speakers turn only about 0.5–2% of their electrical power into sound. The rest becomes heat. The source is older and informal, so treat the figure as a rough estimate.
  | 
  | **TIP**
  | - Cap the volume or gain on a small 0.5W speaker. Both amp modules can output more than 1.8W into 8Ω, which is enough to damage it.
  | 
  | Sources:
  | - [How Speakers Work (diyAudio)](https://www.diyaudio.com/archive/wiki/how_speakers_work.html)
  | - [MIT speaker report](https://web.mit.edu/2.972/www/reports/speaker/speaker.html)
  | - [DFRobot MAX98357 module](https://wiki.dfrobot.com/SKU_DFR0954_MAX98357_I2S_Amplifier_Module)
  | - [PAM8403 amp (Protosupplies)](https://protosupplies.com/product/pam8403-mini-stereo-audio-amp/)

